<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalocsa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kalocsa is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on the plain east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">King Stephen</a> I made it the seat of an archbishopric in the early 11th century, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a>, and its archbishops headed the church across much of southern Hungary. It is known today for its baroque cathedral and archbishop's palace, its paprika and its colourful folk embroidery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalocsa.jpg" alt="Kalocsa" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalocsa_-_Archiepiscopal_palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
